__d(function(a,e,t,n,i,s,r){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 Geography",textbook:"The Earth: Our Habitat \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:19,chapter_title:"Our Country \u2014 India",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the total area of India and what is its rank in the world by size?",back:"India has a total area of about 3.28 million sq km (32.87 lakh sq km). By area, India is the 7th largest country in the world. By population, India is the most populous country in the world (surpassing China in 2023). India occupies about 2.4% of the world's total land area.",memory_hook:"India = 7th by area, 1st by population. Area: 3.28 million sq km \u2014 think '3-2-8'. Population king but area 7th \u2014 a small giant!"},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the latitudinal and longitudinal extents of India?",back:"India lies between latitudes 8\xb04'N and 37\xb06'N and between longitudes 68\xb07'E and 97\xb025'E. The Tropic of Cancer (23\xb030'N) passes through the middle of India, dividing it into the northern temperate zone and the tropical zone. India's latitudinal extent of about 29\xb0 gives it a wide variety of climates \u2014 from tropical in the south to temperate and alpine in the north.",memory_hook:"Latitude: 8\xb0N (south tip, near Sri Lanka) to 37\xb0N (north tip, near Siachen). Longitude: 68\xb0E (west, near Pakistan) to 97\xb0E (east, near Myanmar). The Tropic of Cancer cuts right through the middle \u2014 like India's waistline."},{id:"fc03",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name the countries that share land borders with India.",back:"India shares land borders with seven countries: (1) Pakistan \u2014 northwest; (2) Afghanistan \u2014 northwest (tiny border in J&K); (3) China \u2014 north and northeast; (4) Nepal \u2014 north; (5) Bhutan \u2014 northeast; (6) Bangladesh \u2014 east (shares India's longest land border); (7) Myanmar \u2014 east. India's total land boundary is about 15,200 km. Bangladesh shares the longest border with India.",memory_hook:"Clockwise from NW: Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar. Mnemonic: 'Paki And China Never Bring Back Money' \u2014 Pakistan, Afghanistan, China, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Myanmar."},{id:"fc04",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Which water bodies surround India on three sides?",back:"India is a peninsula \u2014 surrounded by water on three sides: (1) Arabian Sea \u2014 to the west; (2) Bay of Bengal \u2014 to the east; (3) Indian Ocean \u2014 to the south. India has a total coastline of about 7,516 km. The Indian Ocean is named after India, reflecting India's historic maritime importance. India's island territories include the Andaman & Nicobar Islands (Bay of Bengal) and Lakshadweep (Arabian Sea).",memory_hook:"India has a water 'U' shape: Arabian Sea (left), Bay of Bengal (right), Indian Ocean (bottom). Like India is sitting in a water tub \u2014 open at the north (Himalayas) and water on all three other sides."},{id:"fc05",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How many states and Union Territories does India have?",back:"As of 2024, India has 28 states and 8 Union Territories (UTs). The states have elected governments, while UTs are directly administered by the Central Government. The 8 UTs are: Delhi, Jammu & Kashmir, Ladakh, Puducherry, Chandigarh, Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu, Lakshadweep, and Andaman & Nicobar Islands. The most recently created UT is Ladakh (2019).",memory_hook:"28 states + 8 UTs = 36 total administrative units. States have their own government; UTs are run by the Centre. Think: States = self-governing, UTs = centrally managed."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is India's Standard Meridian? Why is it important?",back:"India's Standard Meridian is 82\xb030'E (82 degrees 30 minutes East). It passes through Naini, near Prayagraj (Allahabad), Uttar Pradesh. This meridian was chosen as the standard time reference for all of India (Indian Standard Time \u2014 IST) because it is centrally located in the country. IST is 5 hours 30 minutes ahead of GMT (Greenwich Mean Time). Using one standard time prevents confusion across such a large country.",memory_hook:"82\xb030'E = India's clock meridian. Passes through Prayagraj (UP). IST = GMT + 5:30 hours. When London is 12:00 noon, India is 5:30 PM. India's clock = half an hour ahead of most countries \u2014 uniquely '5:30'!"},{id:"fc07",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the northernmost, southernmost, easternmost, and westernmost point of India?",back:"Northernmost point: Indira Col, Siachen Glacier, Ladakh (37\xb06'N). Southernmost point: Indira Point, Great Nicobar Island (6\xb045'N) \u2014 this is the southernmost tip of Indian territory. Easternmost point: Kibithu (Anjaw district, Arunachal Pradesh). Westernmost point: Sir Creek / Guhar Moti, Gujarat (68\xb07'E). The southernmost point of the mainland (not islands) is Kanyakumari, Tamil Nadu.",memory_hook:"North: Indira Col (mountains, Ladakh). South: Indira Point (island, Nicobar). East: Kibithu (Arunachal). West: Sir Creek (Gujarat). Note: both extreme north and south are named 'Indira' \u2014 a nice memory hook!"},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is India called a 'subcontinent'?",back:"India is called a subcontinent because it is a very large landmass that is distinct from the rest of Asia geographically, culturally, and historically. It is separated from the rest of Asia by the Himalayas to the north and surrounded by seas on three sides. The Indian subcontinent includes India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, Bhutan, and Sri Lanka. The term 'subcontinent' implies it is large enough to be almost a continent, but is part of the larger Asian continent.",memory_hook:"Sub + continent = almost a continent. The Himalayas act like a wall separating India from the rest of Asia \u2014 making India feel like its own world. The subcontinent = India + neighbours enclosed by mountains and seas."},{id:"fc09",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the major physical divisions of India?",back:"India has six major physical divisions: (1) The Himalayan Mountains \u2014 in the north; (2) The Northern Plains \u2014 the fertile Gangetic Plain; (3) The Peninsular Plateau \u2014 the Deccan Plateau; (4) The Indian Desert \u2014 the Thar Desert in Rajasthan; (5) The Coastal Plains \u2014 the narrow strips along the Arabian Sea (west coast) and Bay of Bengal (east coast); (6) The Islands \u2014 Andaman & Nicobar (Bay of Bengal) and Lakshadweep (Arabian Sea).",memory_hook:"India's 6 zones: HiNoPePaDeCo+Is: Himalaya, Northern Plains, Peninsular Plateau, Deserts, Coasts, Islands. From the frozen north to tropical islands \u2014 India has every landscape on Earth within its borders."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Which is the largest state and the smallest state in India by area?",back:"Largest state by area: Rajasthan (342,239 sq km). Smallest state by area: Goa (3,702 sq km). Note: before 2000, Madhya Pradesh was the largest state. Rajasthan became the largest after MP was divided to create Chhattisgarh. Goa is also notable for being India's richest state by per capita income and a major tourism destination.",memory_hook:"Rajasthan = RAJ (large and majestic). Goa = tiny coastal gem. Rajasthan is 92 times the size of Goa \u2014 a remarkable contrast within the same country."},{id:"fc11",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the two main island groups of India? Where are they located?",back:"India has two main island groups: (1) Andaman & Nicobar Islands \u2014 located in the Bay of Bengal, southeast of mainland India. The Andamans have about 572 islands. Port Blair is the capital. India's only active volcano (Barren Island) is here. (2) Lakshadweep \u2014 located in the Arabian Sea, southwest of India. It has 36 tiny coral islands. Kavaratti is its capital. It is India's smallest Union Territory by area (32 sq km).",memory_hook:"Bay of Bengal = Andaman & Nicobar (big, in the east). Arabian Sea = Lakshadweep (tiny coral dots, in the west). AndamanS = east, LakshadwEEp = west. Remember: AN (east) and L (west)."},{id:"fc12",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Karnataka: where is it located in India, and what are its neighbouring states?",back:"Karnataka is located in the southwestern part of peninsular India. It is the 6th largest state by area (191,791 sq km). Neighbouring states: Maharashtra (north), Goa (northwest), Kerala (south), Tamil Nadu (southeast), Andhra Pradesh (east), and Telangana (northeast). Karnataka is bordered to the west by the Arabian Sea (coastline of about 320 km). Bengaluru is Karnataka's capital and India's IT hub.",memory_hook:"Karnataka's neighbours (clockwise from north): Maharashtra \u2192 Telangana \u2192 Andhra \u2192 Tamil Nadu \u2192 Kerala \u2192 Goa. Karnataka is sandwiched between the coast (west) and Deccan Plateau (east). Bengaluru = Silicon Valley of India."},{id:"fc13",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Tropic of Cancer and which Indian states does it pass through?",back:"The Tropic of Cancer is the parallel of latitude at 23\xb030'N, marking the northernmost point where the sun can appear directly overhead (during summer solstice, June 21). It divides India into the tropical zone (south) and the subtropical zone (north). Indian states through which it passes: Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, West Bengal, Tripura, and Mizoram (8 states).",memory_hook:"Tropic of Cancer = 23.5\xb0N = India's waistline. 8 states from west to east: Gujarat \u2192 Rajasthan \u2192 MP \u2192 Chhattisgarh \u2192 Jharkhand \u2192 West Bengal \u2192 Tripura \u2192 Mizoram. Remember: the Tropic bisects India \u2014 states above it are subtropical, below it are tropical."},{id:"fc14",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the length of India's coastline? Which states have the longest and shortest coastlines?",back:"India's total coastline is about 7,516 km (including island coastlines). The state with the longest mainland coastline is Gujarat (1,600 km). Andhra Pradesh has the second longest (974 km). Among states, Goa has the shortest coastline. Karnataka has a coastline of about 320 km along the Arabian Sea \u2014 the Konkan coast district and coastal Karnataka (Dakshina Kannada, Udupi) are famous for seafood cuisine.",memory_hook:"Coastline total = 7,516 km. Gujarat = longest coast state. Karnataka's coast = 320 km of Arabian Sea goodness \u2014 Mangaluru fish curry and Udupi cuisine come from this strip of land."},{id:"fc15",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the significance of the Himalayas for India's climate?",back:"The Himalayas play a crucial role in India's climate: (1) they block cold Arctic winds from Central Asia, preventing northern India from becoming as cold as Tibet or Siberia; (2) they force monsoon winds to rise, causing heavy rainfall in northern India; (3) they are the source of perennial rivers (Ganga, Indus, Brahmaputra) that support agriculture; (4) they act as a natural boundary with China and Tibet. Without the Himalayas, India's climate would be far harsher.",memory_hook:"Himalayas = India's bodyguard + water supplier. They block cold winds FROM the north and force monsoon rains ON the north. Double service \u2014 protection and irrigation \u2014 from the same mountains."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between a state and a Union Territory in India?",back:"A State has its own elected government (Chief Minister + State Legislature) and enjoys considerable autonomy in running its affairs under the Indian Constitution. A Union Territory (UT) is directly administered by the Central Government through a Lieutenant Governor or Administrator appointed by the President. Some UTs like Delhi and Puducherry have their own elected assemblies with limited powers. UTs are usually smaller or strategically sensitive areas.",memory_hook:"State = self-governing (has its own CM and legislature). UT = centrally governed (no elected government in most cases, or limited powers). States are adults who manage their own home; UTs are like children under the central parent government."},{id:"fc17",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name India's neighbouring countries that are islands.",back:"India has two island neighbours: (1) Sri Lanka \u2014 located in the Indian Ocean, separated from India's southeastern tip (Tamil Nadu) by the Palk Strait and Palk Bay. Sri Lanka is connected to India by a chain of small islands called Adam's Bridge (Ram Setu). (2) Maldives \u2014 a group of coral islands in the Indian Ocean, southwest of India. Maldives is India's closest maritime neighbour to the southwest.",memory_hook:"Island neighbours: Sri Lanka (east-south, across Palk Strait from Tamil Nadu) + Maldives (southwest, tiny coral nation). India shares water borders with both. Sri Lanka = connected by Ram Setu (Adam's Bridge)."},{id:"fc18",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Thar Desert? In which state is it located?",back:"The Thar Desert (also called the Great Indian Desert) is located in the state of Rajasthan in northwestern India. It is the world's 7th largest desert. It covers about 200,000 sq km and extends into Pakistan (Sindh province). The Thar receives very little rainfall (less than 25 cm annually) and has extreme temperatures \u2014 very hot in summer (up to 50\xb0C) and cold in winter. The Aravalli Hills on its eastern border prevent moisture from reaching the desert.",memory_hook:"Thar = India's sandy sea. Rajasthan = Rajput land + Thar Desert. The Aravallis block monsoon winds from reaching the Thar \u2014 which is why Rajasthan is dry. Remove the Aravallis and Rajasthan might be green!"},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is India called a 'land of diversity'? Give examples from Karnataka.",back:"India is called a 'land of diversity' because it has enormous variation in: geography (mountains, plains, deserts, coasts, islands), climate (tropical, arid, temperate, alpine), language (22 official languages, 1,600+ dialects), religion (Hinduism, Islam, Christianity, Sikhism, Buddhism, Jainism), and culture (food, dress, festivals). Karnataka alone reflects this diversity \u2014 Kodagu has forests and coffee; Rajasthan has deserts; Darjeeling has tea gardens; the Sundarbans has mangroves.",memory_hook:"India's diversity = 28 states \xd7 completely different geography, language, food, climate. Karnataka alone has 3 distinct zones: coastal (Mangaluru), hilly (Coorg), and plateau (Bengaluru, Mysuru). One state, three worlds."},{id:"fc20",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why did India need a Standard Time even though it spans about 30 degrees of longitude?",back:"India spans from 68\xb07'E to 97\xb025'E \u2014 a difference of about 29 degrees of longitude. Since the Earth rotates 15\xb0 per hour, there is roughly a 2-hour time difference between India's westernmost (Gujarat) and easternmost (Arunachal Pradesh) points. Without a Standard Time, sunrise in Arunachal Pradesh (around 4 AM) would create chaos in scheduling with Gujarat (where sunrise is ~2 hours later). India's Standard Meridian 82\xb030'E and IST (GMT+5:30) provides a uniform time for the entire country.",memory_hook:"29\xb0 difference = nearly 2 hours of sun difference. Without standard time: Arunachal's breakfast time = Gujarat's pre-dawn. Standard time (82\xb030'E) = India's common clock so trains, flights, offices all run together."}]}},2928,[]);